today marks the first day of black  history month a time for celebration and  education of african american culture  authors ibram x kendi and kisha blaine  are releasing a new book called  400 souls a community history of african  america  1619-2019  the anthology features essays from 90  writers and poets  who each focus on a specific period of  time  keisha blaine is an historian and  professor at the university of  pittsburgh  and cbs news contributor ebra max kendy  is the founding director of the boston  university center  for anti-racist research they join us  first  on cbs this morning welcome to you both  congratulations thank you so much for  being here  ibram let me start with you because this  is a book that has as i pointed out  of 90 different authors a variety of  voices  why was it important that you use so  many authors  well in in in 2018 i was  thinking about what would be the best  way to commemorate  this 400th birthday symbolic birthday of  black america this  this this community and as an historian  of course i was  like well let's let's write a history  book  but then the more that i thought about  it the more i realized well why not  bring together a community to write the  history  of a community and and so of course i  had to reach out to to professor blaine  who who was willing to partner with me  um  and and we were able to do that bring  together an incredible community of  writers to write a history of a  community  kisha you say that you wanted to  challenge the idea  that the black community is a monolithic  community  yes i think this is so important because  of course we  may look similar but we're not the same  there's so much diversity of thought and  diversity of experiences and even  diversity backgrounds  and it was important for us to represent  that  richness and complexity in the way that  we told  the history in 400 souls you know i  thought was interesting  number one i want to thank you both for  having short chapters  it was such a great read because you  could zip through it very quickly so i  thank you for that  number two there were so many stories i  didn't know hugh davis was one that  stuck out to me  was a white man who was beaten because  he had slept with a  with a black woman and it that was an  interesting twist in what the stories  that we've been told in the past  why did you include that professor  well yeah it was an incredible essay by  oh yeah you're both professor  sorry  he was beaten for dishonoring whiteness  and nothing happened to her  go ahead professor kennedy first  no i was just going to say very quickly  i mean idioma's piece was  was incredible but i think it really  speaks to  how old racism is  is in what later became america and even  how old black women have been  sort of devalued uh you know in america  yeah that was in 1630. i like to  professor blaine where you said  i am my ancestors wildest dreams that is  absolutely one of my favorite sayings  why is that so powerful  it's so powerful because it speaks to  all that  i think we've overcome certainly as  individuals  but but even collectively as black  people  because certainly if you look back at  the history and you reflect on even the  time period that we're discussing  now you would really have to marvel  at what black people have managed to  accomplish given  uh the experiences of slavery jim crow  and the continued challenges of racism  and white supremacy  another uh story that that i think uh is  not familiar to a lot of people is about  maria stewart who was a feminist  feminist and  an abolitionist in boston in the 1830s  why why did you choose to include her  story  her story is so powerful because it  resonates uh with the present in many  ways especially when we think about  for example the black lives matter  movement we think about the courageous  uh three black women who found  the movement and we saw just in the last  couple of months with the uprisings how  black women have played such a pivotal  role  in shaping american politics and mariah  stewart's story from the 1830s  help us recognize that this is certainly  not a new uh phenomena that black women  have been leading for a very long time  and they have been asserting their  voices  and demanding rights for themselves and  also demand rights for everyone  and as we mark black history month it  always sticks in my call that it's the  shortest month of the year but that's  another story  what do you want people to know what  what should we remember and take from  this month as we begin to honor and  observe and celebrate  professor kendy well i i think this  history book  really demonstrates that there are so  many  individuals historical figures that we  should continue  to revere but we should also not forget  those  ordinary black folks who  have been part of this longer struggle  and so we were very  keen on on bringing in stories of  everyday black folks who  who who strove to be free throughout the  course of their history  yeah i like to think of it as american  history uh professor blaine you want to  take us off  it's american history i think and it's  really important to  remember and and just reflect on the  strength and resilience  of black people in the united states as  people read the book they'll see  the challenges that black people faced  but just the way that they found uh they  were able to overcome those challenges  and the way that they were able  uh to move things forward and really to  make  uh the nation a better place for all of  us even though the work and the struggle  still continues  yeah i'm looking at this thank you both  you said the book shows that black  people never stop dreaming or fighting  for their dreams to become a  reality and certainly some of that  continues today i'm going to hold up the  book if you don't mind  ibra mex kindy and keisha blaine we  thank you both 400 souls goes on sale  tomorrow wherever you like to buy your  books
 

